al.\n\nA fraction is a terminating decimal if and only if its denominator, when the fraction is in lowest terms, has no prime factors other than 2 and 5. Therefore, $1 + 2x$ must be of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$ where $a$ and $b$ are non-negative integers.\n\nSince $x > 1$, the smallest possible value for $x$ is 2. Let's evaluate each possible value of $x$ and check if $1 + 2x$ is of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$:\n\n1. If $x = 2$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 4 = 5 = 5^1$. This is a valid form, so $k = \\frac{1}{5}$.\n2. If $x = 3$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 6 = 7$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n3. If $x = 4$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 8 = 9$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n4. If $x = 5$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 10 = 11$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n5. If $x = 6$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 12 = 13$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n6. If $x = 7$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 14 = 15 = 3 \\cdot 5$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n7. If $x = 8$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 16 = 17$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n8. If $x = 9$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 18 = 19$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n9. If $x = 10$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 20 = 21 = 3 \\cdot 7$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n10. If $x = 12$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 24 = 25 = 5^2$. This is a valid form, so $k = \\frac{1}{25}$.\n11. If $x = 14$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 28 = 29$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n12. If $x = 16$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 32 = 33 = 3 \\cdot 11$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n13. If $x = 18$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 36 = 37$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n14. If $x = 24$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 48 = 49$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n15. If $x = 25$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 50 = 51 = 3 \\cdot 17$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n16. If $x = 28$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 56 = 57 = 3 \\cdot 19$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n17. If $x = 32$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 64 = 65 = 5 \\cdot 13$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n18. If $x = 36$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 72 = 73$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n19. If $x = 48$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 96 = 97$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n20. If $x = 75$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 150 = 151$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n21. If $x = 124$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 248 = 249 = 3 \\cdot 83$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n22. If $x = 249$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 498 = 499$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n23. If $x = 624$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 1248 = 1249$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n24. If $x = 1249$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 2498 = 2499 = 3 \\cdot 833$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n25. If $x = 3124$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 6248 = 6249 = 3 \\cdot 2083$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n26. If $x = 6249$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 12498 = 12499$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n27. If $x = 15624$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 31248 = 31249 = 17 \\cdot 1838$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n28. If $x = 31249$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 62498 = 62499 = 3 \\cdot 20833$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n29. If $x = 78124$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 156248 = 156249 = 3 \\cdot 52083$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n30. If $x = 156249$, then $1 + 2x = 1 + 312498 = 312499 = 17 \\cdot 18383$. This is not of the form $2^a \\cdot 5^b$.\n\nAfter checking all possible values, the only valid values of $k$ are $\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{25}$. The sum of these values is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{25} = \\frac{5}{25} + \\frac{1}{25} = \\frac{6}{25}\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of all possible values of $k$ is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{6}{25}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{6}{25}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
